TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "play-type labeling",
    "AVOID labels",
    "sequential effects of play types",
    "lagged effects",
    "opponent next possession PPP",
    "own-sequence context"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "measuring lagged effects of play types",
    "attribution of play effects to subsequent possessions",
    "confounding factors in sequential basketball analysis",
    "sample size and exposure sparsity for play sequences",
    "derivation of 'AVOID' labels"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior project work."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior project work."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question posits a relationship between the execution of a specific play type ('play X'), its immediate context (own-sequence lags), and subsequent outcomes (opponent next-possession PPP) that influence or 'drive' 'AVOID' labels. The provided data (play-by-play, derived play-type records) appears sufficient to identify play types, sequence possessions, and calculate points per possession (PPP) for both own and opponent possessions. The core challenge lies in the definition and independence of 'AVOID labels' and adequately controlling for confounding factors inherent in sequential basketball data."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition and source of 'AVOID labels' are not provided. It is unclear if these are pre-existing, externally defined labels, or if they are meant to be derived from the very effects being studied (e.g., 'AVOID' a play if its lagged PPP is high). If the latter, the question becomes definitional rather than an empirical test of a hypothesis.",
    "The specific 'own-sequence lags' to be considered are not defined (e.g., immediately preceding play, average of last N plays, specific combinations)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available to influence the decision regarding this research question."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.7,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
        "text": "If 'AVOID labels' are directly derived from high opponent next-possession PPP or similar lagged effects, the question becomes circular and defines the label rather than tests an independent hypothesis. This would make the question untestable as an empirical claim."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Specific play types, especially when considering sequential effects (e.g., 'play X' followed by 'play Y' leading to a certain opponent response), may have limited occurrences, leading to insufficient sample sizes for robust statistical analysis and low power to detect effects."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior project work was retrieved that directly addresses this research question."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "The primary risk is that 'AVOID labels' are not independently defined but are instead derived from the very lagged PPP effects the research aims to test. This would render the hypothesis tautological or definitional rather than an empirical investigation."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Many factors influence opponent next-possession PPP and subsequent play calling (e.g., score differential, game clock, personnel changes, previous possession outcome, specific defensive schemes, momentum shifts). Isolating the effect of 'play X' and its own-sequence lags requires careful control for these confounders."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The frequency of specific play types, especially in particular sequential contexts or against specific opponents, might be too low to achieve adequate statistical power for detecting meaningful effects. This is particularly true for 'own-sequence lags' which consider patterns of multiple consecutive plays."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The 'derived play-type possession records' are a critical input. Their accuracy, consistency, and how they define 'initiator' and 'play type' directly impact the validity of the analysis. Any unreliability or error in these derived labels could propagate."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Basketball strategies and player tendencies can evolve over seasons. Aggregating data across multiple seasons (2022-23 to 2025-26) might obscure non-stationary effects, or a play's impact could change over time."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.3,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.2,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of running 'play X' (and specific 'own-sequence lags' preceding it) on the expected Points Per Possession (PPP) of the immediate subsequent opponent possession, and whether this effect is significantly associated with (or predicts) 'AVOID labels' assigned to 'play X'.",
    "unit": "A single offensive possession for the team running 'play X', linked to the subsequent opponent possession.",
    "sample": "All offensive possessions from the NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs, and the complete 2025-26 regular season for which 'play X' can be identified. Possessions must have a clearly identifiable subsequent opponent possession. Subsamples will be created for specific 'play X' types and 'own-sequence lags'.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted for key 'play X' types and 'own-sequence lag' patterns. Minimum detectable effect sizes for PPP differences will be established. If sample sizes for specific sequences are too low to achieve adequate power for a reasonable effect size, those specific sequences will be noted as untestable.",
    "controls": "Statistical models (e.g., regression with fixed effects or propensity score matching) will control for: game state (score differential, quarter, remaining clock), team/player personnel on court (for both teams), opponent strength, outcome of 'play X' itself (e.g., turnover, made shot, free throws), and previous possession outcome. Player-level fixed effects or hierarchical models may be used to account for player-specific tendencies and play execution quality. The definition of 'AVOID labels' must be established *before* analysis and confirmed to be independent of the PPP outcome being measured.",
    "falsification": "A falsification test will involve analyzing the effect of 'play X' on opponent PPP for possessions that are not immediately subsequent but are, for example, two or three possessions later. No significant effect is expected at these longer lags, providing a check against general team strength or game-level confounding. Another test will be to examine if 'AVOID labels' are predicted by random noise or unrelated game events.",
    "multiplicity": "Adjustments for multiple comparisons will be applied if analyzing numerous 'play X' types or 'own-sequence lag' patterns simultaneously, using methods like Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate (FDR) control.",
    "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be considered supported if, after controlling for confounders, a statistically significant (p < 0.05) and practically meaningful (e.g., >0.05 PPP difference) increase in opponent next-possession PPP is consistently observed after 'play X' or specific 'own-sequence lags', and this effect is strongly associated with independently defined 'AVOID labels'. If 'AVOID labels' are found to be derived from the outcome, the question will be deemed untestable as originally posed.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, and NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete as of 2026-04-13), utilizing the derived play-type possession records and raw play-by-play data."
  }
}

